 { Next, let's see some examples on the topic of transposing to a pawn ending with a decisive outside passed pawn. } 1. { -KEY- } Bc3 $1 { Fischer has seen that, after the exchange of bishops, his outside passed pawn on a4 will decide. } ( { Less convincing was } 1. Bb4 Ke8 2. a5 Kd7 3. a6 Kc6 4. a7 Kb7 { since Black could try to reach a theoretical draw by eliminating the kingside pawns; e.g. } 5. Bc5 Be5 $1 6. Ke4 Bc7 { followed by ... h5-h4 and a bishop sacrifice for the remaining pawn. } ) 
Bxc3 ( { Or } 1... Ba3 2. a5 Bc5 3. Bd4 $1 { insisting on the plan - Speelman. } ) 
2. Kxc3 Ke7 3. Kd4 $1 { The white king immediately starts an attack on the enemy pawns. } Kd6 4. a5 f6 5. a6 $1 Kc6 6. a7 Kb7 7. Kd5 h4 8. Ke6 ( { Black resigned, since } 8. Ke6 f5 9. Kf6 { is hopeless for him. } ) 

