 { 'A knight ending is in fact a pawn ending' said Botvinnik - or something similar - although Speelman disagrees with this statement in his interesting work 'Analysing the Endgame'. Whoever is right, the following example shows that the value of an outside passed pawn increases drastically once the rooks leave the board. } 1... { -KEY- } Rd7 $1 { Swapping the active enemy rook, which also tends to be the best piece at controlling outside passed pawns such as the one on a5. } 2. Rxd7 Nxd7 3. Nd6 Ke7 4. Nc4 a4 5. Kf1 Ke6 6. Ke2 Kd5 { Without knights on the board this would be a trivial win, similar to the one arising in the previous example. Here White can still put up some resistance, counting on the tactical potential of the knight. } 7. Ne3+ ( { Or } 7. Na3 Kd4 { ( } Ne5 { is less convincing due to } 8. f4 $1 Nc4 9. Nb5 a3 10. Kd3 $1 { followed by 11.Na3(+), as observed by Skyrolla) } 8. Kd2 Ne5 { in similar fashion to the game. } ) 
Kd4 8. Kd2 ( { After } 8. Nc2 Kc3 9. Na3 Nf6 10. f4 Ne4 $1 11. g4 Nd6 { threatening ...Kb2, Black wins - Kasparov. } ) 
Ne5 9. Kc2 Nd3 10. Nd1 Ne1+ 11. Kb2 Nf3 $1 { After deflecting the enemy pieces to the queenside, Black proceeds with an attack on the opposite flank. } 12. h4 Ne5 13. Ka3 ( { Before the king enters on e4-f3, Black arranges to meet } 13. Ne3 { with } Nc4 $1 ) 
Ke4 14. h5 ( { Seeing that } 14. Kxa4 Kf3 { followed by ...Ng4xf2 is hopeless for him, Schmidt opts for active defence. } ) 
gxh5 15. Nc3+ ( { Or } 15. Kxa4 Kf3 16. Kb3 Nd3 17. Kc2 Nxf2 18. Nc3 Nh1 $1 { and wins - Kasparov; } ( { but not } 18. Nc3+ Ne4 $4 19. Nxe4 Kxe4 20. Kd2 { with a draw. } ) 
 ) 
Kf3 16. Nd5 Ng4 $1 { Preventing any attempts at counterplay with 17.Nf6 } 17. Ne7 ( { Or } 17. Nf4 Nf6 18. Nd3 Ke2 { etc - Kasparov. } ) 
Nxf2 18. Nf5 Ne4 { The rest is easy. } 19. Kxa4 Nxg3 20. Nh4+ Ke4 21. Kb4 Nf5 $1 
 0-1

