 ( { Several factors speak in White's favour: superior minor piece, better pawn structure and a faster activation route for his king. Probably there was nothing wrong with the simple } 0... Kf6 0. Kd2 { , heading for the opposing queenside pawns, but Predojevic found a more clear-cut path to victory. } ) 
1. { -KEY- } Bxe6 $1 { Transposing to a pawn ending where a potential outside passed pawn on the kingside will soon decide matters. } fxe6 2. Kd2 Kf7 ( { Or } 2... f5 3. Kd3 Kf7 4. Kc4 { winning - Predojevic. } ) 
3. Kd3 Ke7 ( 3... h5 $1 { as observed by ronschaffer would have been a better choice. Instead of defending the queenside, Black prepares kingside counterplay once White's king moves towards the b-pawn, for instance, } 4. Kc4 Kg6 5. Kb5 e5 $1 { (only now, when White's king can't reach e4 anymore) } 6. fxe5 fxe5 7. Kc4 Kf5 8. Kd5 ( 7... Ke7 7. h3 $2 e4 9. Kd4 h4 $1 { even wins for Black due to a mating motif in the upcoming queen endgame. } ) 
