 { Finally, let's see two examples where the idea of creating an outside passed pawn wasn't properly anticipated by its opponent, a situation we already came across in Schlechter-Tartakower.Thanks to his more active rook White is better, but after the solid 1...Re7 it would not have been easy for him to make progress - Deviatkin. In the game Black permitted the transposition to a pawn ending, where our main theme - the outside passed pawn - will soon play a key role. } 1... { -KEY- } Re4 $2 2. Rc8+ Kf7 3. Rc7+ $1 Re7 ( { Objectively speaking, shedding a pawn but keeping the rooks with } 3... Ke6 4. Rxa7 f4 { was Black's lesser evil, but of course that wasn't what Nisipeanu had in mind. } ) 
4. Rxe7+ Kxe7 5. Kf3 { In Deviatkin's words: 'We are now in a pawn ending - the form of ending which looks so simple, but which is one of the most complicated! Material is equal and Black's position looks okay, but in fact Black is already lost. The spoiled structure on the kingside ruins him, because there is no way for Black to prevent the creation of an outside passed pawn. And if Black's king goes to the queenside, White will be faster.' In Chapter Seven we will see further proof of the fact that transpositions to pawn endings usually imply dangers for the defender. } Kd6 ( { Or } 5... Ke6 6. Kf4 g5 7. Kf3 Ke5 8. h3 $1 Kd5 9. g3 Ke5 10. h4 { with similar play. } ) 
6. Kf4 Kd5 ( { As Deviatkin already mentioned, White wins the passed pawn race: } 6... Kc5 7. h4 Kb4 8. e4 ( { An excellent alternative is } 7... Kd5 7. h5 $5 gxh5 9. Kxf5 { , preparing to queen on e8 with check, as noticed by nayoncohr. } ) 
fxe4 9. Kxe4 f5 { (or } Kxa4 10. g4 { ) } 10. Ke5 Kxa4 11. Kf6 Kxb5 12. Kxg6 a5 13. h5 { and queens, controlling the a1-square. } ) 
7. h4 Ke6 8. g3 Ke7 9. e4 $1 ( { Sasikiran continues with the plan of creating an outside passed pawn, without getting tricked into } 9. h5 $6 Kf7 $1 10. hxg6 Kxg6 ) 
fxe4 10. Kxe4 Kd6 ( { Or } 10... f5 11. Ke5 Kf7 12. Kd6 $1 g5 $5 13. hxg5 Kg6 14. Kc7 Kxg5 15. Kb7 Kg4 16. Kxa7 Kxg3 17. a5 $1 { and White queens with check - Deviatkin. } ) 
11. g4 Ke6 12. h5 Kf7 ( { a) After } 12... gxh5 13. gxh5 ) 
 ( { b) or } 12... f5 13. gxf5 gxf5 14. Kf4 { White's win would be even more trivial. } ) 
13. Kd5 Kg7 14. Ke6 $1 ( { a) Sidestepping the last trick: } 14. Kc6 $4 f5 $1 15. gxf5 gxh5 ) 
 ( { b) After } 14. Ke6 { Black resigned, in view of } f5 15. gxf5 gxh5 16. Ke7 ) 

