 { Even in endings with opposite-coloured bishops, the outside passed pawn remains a relevant factor, as in the next example.Thanks to his more active pieces Black is minimally better, but he was suddenly helped by a bad decision from his opponent. } 1. { -KEY- } axb6 $6 { As Botvinnik himself stated, this capture yields Black the possibility of creating an outside passed pawn later on. } ( 1. Bf2 { seems simpler, followed by Re1, swapping the active enemy rook. } ) 
cxb6 2. Kf2 Kf7 ( { After } 2... Re2 $6 3. Kg1 { the rook would have to return to e6. } ) 
3. Re1 Rxe1 4. Kxe1 ( { Now after } 4. Kxe1 b5 $6 5. Kf2 { a win is unlikely, but Botvinnik has other intentions. } ) 
a5 $1 { Creating the outside passed pawn. } 5. bxa5 ( { Unfortunately for White, after } 5. Bxb6 a4 6. Kd2 f4 { followed by ...Bf1, Black would soon be able to create a second passed pawn on the kingside. } ) 
bxa5 6. g3 a4 7. Kd2 $6 ( 7. Bc5 { was preferable, controlling the passed pawn as soon as possible - and with the bishop, so that the king can be used for the defence of the opposite flank. } ) 
a3 8. Kc2 h5 9. h4 $2 { A tactical oversight which costs the game. } ( { a) After } 9. f4 $2 h4 $1 10. gxh4 ( { or } 10. Bf2 g4 ) 
gxf4 { Black is also winning - Botvinnik. His next step would be a king transfer to assist the advance of the f-pawn. } ) 
 ( { b) But } 9. g4 $1 { yielded good drawing chances - Benko, who explains that White's idea is to swap two pair of pawns on the kingside and then sacrifice the bishop for the remaining one, so that a theoretical draw arises on the opposite flank. There can follow } fxg4 ( { or } 9. h4 hxg4 10. fxg4 $1 f4 11. Bf2 { followed by h3-h4; } ) 
10. hxg4 $1 h4 11. Bf2 Bd5 12. f4 h3 13. Bg3 { and White holds - Benko. Nevertheless, such ideas are much easier to spot after, rather than during, the game. Thus from a practical point of view, 1.axb6? was White's main mistake. } ) 
f4 $1 { A typical breakthrough to create a passed pawn. } 10. Be5 ( { a) After } 10. gxf4 gxh4 11. f5 h3 12. Be5 a2 13. Kb2 h4 { Black should also be winning, using the c4-square as a junction in the king's route to the h-pawns. } ) 
 ( { b) As for } 10. Bf2 { Botvinnik suggests } gxh4 11. gxh4 Ke6 { followed by ...Kf5 and ...Bd5xf3, in order to create a new passed pawn on the f-file. There can follow } 12. Bc5 a2 13. Kb2 Bd5 14. Bf2 Kf5 $1 15. Be1 Bxf3 16. Kxa2 Ke4 { and the king reaches e2 with decisive effect. } ) 
Ke6 $1 { The king wins a tempo on his way to the defence of the f4-pawn. } 11. Bc7 gxh4 12. Bxf4 ( { Or } 12. gxh4 Kf5 { followed by ...Bd5, with similar play as after 10.Bf2. } ) 
h3 13. g4 h4 $1 ( { Avoiding } 13... hxg4 $2 14. fxg4 { and White escapes to a theoretical draw (cf. 9 g4 above). } ) 
14. Bh2 Be2 ( { There could have followed } 14... Be2 15. Kb3 Bxf3 16. g5 Kf5 17. Kxa3 Kxg5 18. Kb4 Kf5 19. Kc5 Ke4 { with ...Be2 and ...Kf3-g2 next - Benko. } ) 

 0-1

